        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<P>

     This experiment had two objectives: 1) to evaluate whether space flight affected the pattern and developmental progression of embryogenically competent daylily
     cells from one well-defined stage to another; and 2) to determine whether mitosis and chromosome behavior were modified by
     the space environment. 
<P>
     APPROACH:
<P>
     Embryogenically competent cell clusters from 200-400 screened fractions of daylily (Hemerocallis cv. Autumn Blaze)
     suspension cultures were grown in plant cell culture chambers obtained from the Japanese Space Agency (NASDA). Cultures
     of embryogenic cells were distributed on a semi-solid nutrient culture medium in NASDA metal petri dishes at Stony Brook,
     transported in a reduced-temperature cooler to Hangar L at Kennedy Space Center and loaded into the Shuttle the day
     before lift-off. An activated-charcoal filter-paper disc inside each dish provided a means of adsorbing any deleterious
     compounds to remove them from the vicinity of the developing cells. It had been determined from ground-based studies that
     the samples would not progress significantly in their embryonic growth and development until orbit was achieved. One plant
     cell culture dish was devoted to each species during the 8-day space flight. Living somatic embryos were returned for
     continued postflight evaluation, development and "grow-out" operations. 
<P>
     RESULTS:
<P>
     The somatic embryos successfully developed; however, the number of units that progressed to later stages of embryo
     development was diminished in flight samples, and the number of cells in division (after landing) was consistently lower in flight
     samples than in controls. Significant alterations (aberrations in chromosome structure) were found in the karyotypes of the
     space-flight materials, but not in the ground controls. A substantial number of inflight samples that are normally uninucleate,
     possessed binucleate cells. Ground control samples were uniformly uninucleate. Since the methodology for karyotype analysis
     involves treatment of cells and somatic embryos with colchicine, care was taken to eliminate the possibility of an increased
     sensitivity of the flight-exposed cells to colchicine, leading to a doubling of the nuclei. Serial sampling and examination of flight
     samples after recovery indicated that the number of binucleate cells in some samples diminished but was not eliminated.
<P>
        </div>

